// What is left of a piece of plant food an animal had started eating, put
// back down. Open bug §Z33 (`Reports/open-bugs-handoff.md`): a forager chews
// its load on the way home, is paid for every bite as it goes, and used to
// put the half-eaten fruit down as a whole fruit -- so the next animal to pick
// it up was paid for the eaten half again. About half of what a forage colony
// ate was food that had already been eaten once.
//
// **Why a material of its own, rather than the fruit keeping a smaller
// worth.** A loose fruit, leaf or flower is `Plant`-kind, and a loose
// plant-kind cell is structural: `structural.rs` writes its support distance
// into `Cell::aux`, the one slot a cell could keep a worth in. A windfall is a
// powder but carries a seed organism, whose cell type lives there too. So the
// remainder goes down as this, a powder no organism ever owns, which keeps
// exactly what was left in `aux` (`carries_worth`) and is priced from it by
// `creature::food_value`. A whole cell still goes down as itself.
//
// **Not meat, whatever it was cut from.** `carries_worth` is the half of
// `worth_in_aux` that says "my worth is in `aux`" without the half that every
// meat census and the energy ledger read as "I am meat". Only plant food
// (`food_class` below 0) is put down as crumbs; flesh still goes down as its
// own material.
//
// **It does not rot**, because the fruit it replaces does not: a half-eaten
// fruit in a nest must not be a new way for food to vanish that a whole one is
// not. Crumbs are eaten or they stay.
//
// **And it does not roll** (`rolls: false`), for the same reason: the fruit
// never moved. As an ordinary powder a crumb put down at a tunnel mouth slid
// down the tunnel and packed it from the bottom, sealed in the lining where
// no ant goes -- the owner spotted it from the first picture. It still drops
// straight down through open air.
(
    name: "crumbs",
    display: "Crumbs",
    kind: Powder,
    density: 0.8,
    friction_angle: 40.0,
    // Chewed pulp: duller and browner than `fruit` or `windfall`, so a nest
    // floor shows where food was part-eaten and put back.
    colors: [
        (150, 96, 60),
        (128, 80, 50),
        (170, 118, 72),
        (110, 70, 48),
    ],
    flammability: 0.3,
    burn_temperature: 480.0,
    burn_duration: 30,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.2,
    insubstantial: true,
    falls_through_organisms: true,
    // **The fallback, never the price.** A crumbs cell is always written with
    // a worth of at least 1 in `aux` (`Carried::into_cell`), and
    // `food_value` reads that first; this is what one would be worth if
    // something ever wrote one bare. A pip's worth, so a bare one is a morsel
    // rather than a feast.
    food_energy: 40.0,
    carries_worth: true,
    rolls: false,
    penetration_resistance: 0.2,
    food_class: -1.0,
)
